{
  "id": "urn:visionflow:page:a6165d3e89a0f5333c88ae7c44537c14570f61efaf22a24a7eef46ae079f6d93",
  "title": "Human Pose SLAM Capture System",
  "slug": "human-pose-slam-capture-system",
  "public": true,
  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:human-pose-slam-capture-system",
  "domain": "spatial-computing",
  "definition": "A Human Pose SLAM Capture System is an integrated sensing and computation pipeline that simultaneously localises a device within an unknown environment (SLAM) while continuously tracking the full-body skeletal pose of one or more human occupants in real time. It fuses data from depth cameras, inertial measurement units, and RGB imagery through probabilistic state estimation \u2014 typically particle filters or factor-graph optimisers \u2014 to produce a joint world model of both the static scene geometry and dynamic human kinematics. The output drives applications including markerless motion capture, avatar animation in extended reality, safety-aware robot navigation around people, and persistent spatial AI anchoring. The discipline sits at the intersection of computer vision, human-computer interaction, and spatial computing, with maturing industrial deployments in XR headsets, telepresence rigs, and autonomous vehicle pedestrian tracking.",
